<%- include('../header.ejs') %>
<%- include('../nav.ejs') %>

<div class="col-12">
  <h1>Weekly tables post</h1>
  <p class="text-muted">
    Exactly what the Saturday post will contain. Nothing on this page sends anything.
  </p>

  <div class="alert <%= facebookConfigured ? 'alert-success' : 'alert-danger' %>">
    <strong>Facebook:</strong>
    <%= facebookConfigured
      ? 'configured — the two tables go up as one album.'
      : 'NOT configured. Set META_TAMESIDE_PAGE_ID and META_TAMESIDE_PAGE_TOKEN on the service.' %>
  </div>

  <div class="alert <%= instagramConfigured ? 'alert-success' : 'alert-warning' %>">
    <strong>Instagram:</strong>
    <% if (instagramConfigured) { %>
      configured — the two tables go up as one carousel.
    <% } else { %>
      not configured (<code>META_IG_USER_ID</code> is unset), so this posts to Facebook only.
      That is a supported setup, not a fault — Tameside's tables have never been on Instagram.
    <% } %>
  </div>

  <div class="alert alert-warning">
    <strong>Before unpausing the scheduler job:</strong> the Make.com scenario still posts
    Tameside's tables to <em>this same Facebook page</em> every Saturday. Two live posters
    means two posts. Disable the Make scenario and unpause the job on the same day, and do
    not let that span a Saturday. Instagram is safe either way now — Make posts to the
    Stockport account, this posts to Tameside's own.
  </div>

  <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Images</h2>
  <p class="text-muted">
    Rendered on demand as JPEG. <strong>Meta fetches these URLs itself, later, from its own
    servers</strong> — so they have to be public, and they have to still work minutes after
    the post is made. If one of these is broken here, it is broken for Meta too.
  </p>
  <div class="row">
    <% images.forEach(function (src) { %>
      <div class="col-12 col-md-6 mb-3">
        <img src="<%= src %>" class="img-fluid border" alt="League table">
        <p class="small text-break mt-1"><a href="<%= src %>"><%= src %></a></p>
      </div>
    <% }); %>
  </div>

  <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Captions</h2>
  <div class="row">
    <div class="col-12 col-lg-6 mb-3">
      <h3 class="h6">Facebook</h3>
      <pre class="border p-2 bg-light"><%= captions.facebook %></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="col-12 col-lg-6 mb-3">
      <h3 class="h6">Instagram</h3>
      <pre class="border p-2 bg-light"><%= captions.instagram %></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="text-muted">
    <% if (captions.mentioned.length) { %>
      Clubs mentioned on Instagram: <%= captions.mentioned.join(', ') %>.
    <% } else { %>
      <strong>No clubs are mentioned</strong>, because no club has an Instagram handle stored
      against it. Add them on the club admin pages and they appear here automatically —
      hardcoding a handle into the caption is how the old automation ended up mentioning a
      stranger. Facebook page mentions are omitted on purpose: they need the Pages API, and
      plain <code>@Club Name</code> text does nothing at all.
    <% } %>
  </p>

  <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Send it</h2>
  <p class="text-muted">
    A dry run creates unpublished Instagram containers to ask Meta whether it would accept
    each image, then abandons them. Nothing becomes visible, and they expire in 24 hours.
  </p>
  <form method="post" action="/admin/social/weekly-tables?dry=1" class="d-inline">
    <button type="submit" class="btn btn-secondary">Dry run (posts nothing)</button>
  </form>
  <form method="post" action="/admin/social/weekly-tables" class="d-inline ms-2"
        onsubmit="return confirm('This posts publicly to Facebook and Instagram now. Continue?');">
    <button type="submit" class="btn btn-danger">Post now</button>
  </form>
</div>

<%- include('../footer.ejs') %>
